  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:vXHDbKKuXQ0",
    "purpose": "A one-year progress update on a pair of Levi's 501 Shrink-to-Fit raw denim jeans, showing their modifications, repairs, fit, fades and wear damage.",
    "video_type": "long_term_review",
    "thesis": "After about a year (roughly six to seven months of steady wear), cheap mass-produced Levi's 501 STF raw denim is wearing and fading about as expected for a $30–50 jean, and the creator plans to keep wearing and repairing them.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The jeans had been tapered earlier; the stitches were removed to return them to a straight leg, leaving visible fade lines on the sides.",
      "A seam-ripper accident on the cuffs was patched with fabric from old brown pants, to be replaced later with a proper denim patch.",
      "The jeans cost about $30, are lighter-weight denim (he believes around 12 oz), and were bought in a 29 waist and 30 length, a size that was very tight at first but now fits well.",
      "He says he would have bought a 32 length, and he cuffs them.",
      "They were worn for runs and walks; fades are vintage-style rather than high contrast, with good whiskers and honeycombs and some yellowing.",
      "Wear damage includes a phone-pocket rip, holes in the thin pocket bags and thinning holes in the crotch.",
      "He plans self-repairs and more frequent washing to make them last longer."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
